/* a frame is a window onto another document; painting it can only board it up */
iframe:not(#sk-never) {
    background-color: transparent !important;
}

/* ⚠ ...and so is a drawing surface, onto whatever it does not draw. A <canvas> is
   transparent everywhere its script has drawn nothing and an <svg> everywhere its
   shapes are not; the element's own ground is never the drawing, it is what shows
   through it. A mapping site is where that boards the picture up: the tiles are
   <img>s absolutely positioned inside zero-sized boxes, and laid across them at the
   full size of the viewport are a <canvas> of labels and a <svg> of outlines. Only
   `bg all` reaches either — neither is a div, a block or text — and painted, each
   alone is an opaque sheet over every tile. Transparent rather than the image grey:
   the grey is there so a picture's ink is legible whichever way it runs, and a
   surface that draws nothing is meant to be seen through. It costs nothing wherever
   the blankets have run, an unpainted box showing its ancestor's black. */
canvas:not(#sk-never), svg:not(#sk-never) {
    background-color: transparent !important;
}

